  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Thursday)\nIssued at 1144 AM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nUpper trough will remain in place through early next week over \nthe western CONUS, supporting mainly WSW flow aloft. Pattern \nchange expected toward next weekend as a strong NE Pac upper \ntrough dives into the Pac NW and supports a couple stronger upper \ntroughs and associated sfc low's across southern Canada and the \nnorthern CONUS. Lingering monsoon wave will phase with a stronger \nupper trough moving across southern Canada today into Sat. Enough \ndiurnal instability will be in place to support isolated to \nscattered showers/storms in the SW FA. However, the better chances\nfor rain will be to the east of the FA where deeper moisture is \nin place. Associated cool front will move through Sat with breezy \nNW winds developing over western SD for a bit. Drier air will move\ninto the region, supporting dry weather through most of Sunday \nbefore the next upper trough and batch of tropical moisture \nadvects into the region. Upper trough will drop into the SW CONUS \nSunday and direct tropical moisture NE across the Rockies. The \npath of this moisture and the location of best forcing is still \nnot settled in forecast models and will hinge on the upper closed \nlow's track, which are difficult to predict for forecast models. \nHence, rain chances are likely to change in the next few model \ncycles. The best window for rain will be Sunday night into Monday,\nwith things drying out after. Slightly cooler, but seasonal the \nrest of the week. Models are still indicating a stronger cold \nfront next weekend, which could bring a widespread frost as the \nupper trough over the NE Pac shifts east and forces cold air south\nout of Canada. However, the degree of CAA with this system varies\nsubstantially among the various forecast models and ensembles.",
